out  that for a second we'll watch her  explain it and then we'll dig into it  take a look  a little few things that we can do one  of them is that we could increase the  taxes that people are paying who are the  extremely wealthy in our communities so  70% 80% we've had it as high as 90% the  other thing that we can do is we can tax  corporations or or have them level out  the gap that that exists is with their  workers I'm also one that really looks  at the defense budget that we have that  has increased in nearly 50% since 9/11  and so most of the money that we have in  there it's much more than we spend on  education on health care I mean I think  what picks up for a lot of people is  taxing the rich up to 80 90 percent or  70 degree and you know 1% was paying a  fair share yeah so the thing there is  the 1% must pay tax rates up to 90  percent I believe that this is simply  bad policy informed by or maybe better  said uninformed by a real knowledge of  the facts and I'll tell you exactly what  I'm talking about in 2018 to be in the  top 1% of the United States you needed  to earn 421 thousand dollars okay 421  thousand dollars puts you in the top 1%  so the suggestion here implicitly is  that she would at least consider a  policy where once you get to the top 1%  or earnings of 421 thousand dollars you  start to pay as much as 90 percent tax  on income above that now remember many  states have income taxes like for  example Massachusetts of 5.3 percent so  under that idea if you live in  Massachusetts and make over 400 21,000  you pay ninety five point three percent  marginal tax rate on all of your income  above that threshold now I know the  defense's people are going to send to me  David she was spitballing 70 80 or 90  percent she didn't mean just the 1%  maybe the tax brackets are different I  believe that this is a missed  both in spirit and by the letter and I'm  willing to be led here by the  peer-reviewed research on this issue I  have not seen evidence that starting a  90 percent marginal tax bracket at 421  thousand dollars is what we need to be  doing now there will be people who say  David hold on there's precedent during  the Eisenhower era ninety-one percent  marginal tax rate well yes yes and no in  the 19 in 1950 it's true that there was  a ninety-one percent marginal tax rate  on incomes over two hundred thousand  dollars number one that's equivalent to  two million today not the 1% right not  four hundred and twenty one thousand two  million inflation adjusted that's just a  fraction of the top one percent you're  not in the 1% today if you earn two  million you're way way way above that  but the more important thing is that  that ninety-one percent marginal tax  rate during the Eisenhower era was  combined with a bunch of other rules  that exempted a lot of income from the  ninety-one percent rate so the effective  tax rates for a lot of people were  actually lower than they are today  even in high tax brackets because a lot  of those exemptions so my challenge to  Alanna Mar is explained to me the data  behind a 90 percent marginal tax rate on  incomes in the 1 percent meaning  starting at 421 thousand dollars what I  would support once again is what I've  seen backed by research and data which  is we need higher tax brackets right now  the highest bracket and starts at I  think it's five hundred and ten thousand  dollars or five hundred and twenty  thousand dollars we need higher brackets  I don't know if there needs to be a  bracket at a million and five and ten  million or what it needs to be we need  higher brackets I've seen no data that  ninety percent is the right number for  that top marginal tax rate I've seen no  data that we should be trying to collect  that rate from people in the four  hundred thousand dollar income range so  what I would support is a system that's  progressive with a high marginal rate  around 70 percent for the top of that  one percent and scaling up from the I  believe